meantime Iran's supreme leader ayatollah  khamenei ii is accusing the country's  enemies of causing the largest protests  against the islamic regime in nearly a  decade according to Iranian state TV 9  more people including a child died  overnight so far the violence has  claimed at least 21 lives more than 400  have been arrested Elizabeth Palmer has  reported from Iran many times and she is  monitoring the protests from London  Elizabeth good morning good morning well  this all started last Thursday with one  isolated protest in a northern city  called Mashhad and it was about food  prices and economic hardship but such as  the pent-up frustration in Iran that  overnight these protests mushroomed and  morphed until now they've affected more  than 50 cities in Tehran crowds  vandalized one of the main shopping  avenues and in city after city people  have come out to protest jeer and throw  stones at the security forces police  have chased demonstrators away with  water cannons in some areas and there  have been isolated reports of shooting  impossible to verify by both the  security forces and the demonstrators  the protesters especially the young and  the working poor are angry about  everything from corruption and  unemployment to lack of freedom and  religious rule they've even been  vandalizing pictures of supreme leader  Ayatollah Khamenei II which is the  ultimate act of defiance in Iran  president Hassan rouhani who's a  moderate went on TV on New Year's Eve  and his response was surprisingly mild  he said people had a right to protest as  long as there was no violence but other  hardline voices are now calling for a  crackdown the authorities are desperate  to get this unrest under control but a  brutal response like the one we saw in  2009 where security forces shot people  in the streets could add fuel to the  fire and turn this into a bloodbath now  I've been speaking to contacts of ours  in Iran this morning they say that in  the cap  things are quiet but very tense  thousands of police on the streets both  uniformed and undercover normally over  the past few days the demonstrations  been getting going around five o'clock  so it's too early to tell whether this  is going to spread even further are  start to fizzle out but of course the  whole world is watching Alex it is  Elizabeth thanks
 

